I've only read files so far; nothing is edited. The main thing that shapes the plan is how login starts a session in this tree: `createSession(user.id)`, with one argument (`src/api/routes.ts:10`). An earlier version of this signup route, in commit `abbc153`, called `createSession(user.id, { ttlMs })` instead. That two-argument version no longer exists here because the demo seed commit `81aee1c` removed it along with the old route and test. So I'll match login exactly.

## Plan

### 1. `src/api/account.ts`
Widen the imports:
```ts
import { createUser, getUser, publicUser, UserError } from "../auth/users.ts";
import { createSession, revokeSession } from "../auth/session.ts";
```
Add this entry to `accountRoutes`, between `/account` and `/api/logout`:
```ts
  {
    method: "POST",
    path: "/api/signup",
    handler: (req) => {
      const email = field(req.body, "email");
      const name = field(req.body, "name") ?? "";
      const password = field(req.body, "password");
      if (!email || !password) return json(400, { error: "email and password are required" });
      try {
        const user = createUser(email, name, password);
        const session = createSession(user.id);
        return json(201, { token: session.token, user: publicUser(user) });
      } catch (err) {
        if (err instanceof UserError) return json(400, { error: err.message });
        throw err;
      }
    },
  },
```
- **Optional `name`:** a missing `name` is allowed because `createUser` already uses the email as the name when the name is empty.
- **Missing fields:** a missing `email` or `password` gets a 400, the same as login.
- **Other errors:** anything that isn't a `UserError` is re-thrown, and the router turns it into a 500.

### 2. `test/signup.test.ts`
```ts
import { test, beforeEach } from "node:test";
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { app } from "../src/api/routes.ts";
import { createUser, resetUsers } from "../src/auth/users.ts";
import { resetSessions } from "../src/auth/session.ts";

const call = (method: string, path: string, body?: unknown, token?: string) =>
  app({ method, path, body, headers: token ? { authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } : {} });

beforeEach(() => {
  resetUsers();
  resetSessions();
});

test("signup creates the member and signs them in", async () => {
  const res = await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "Grace@Example.com", name: "Grace", password: "hopper1906" });
  assert.equal(res.status, 201);
  const { token, user } = res.body as { token: string; user: { id: string; email: string; name: string } };
  assert.deepEqual(user, { id: user.id, email: "grace@example.com", name: "Grace" });
  assert.ok(!("passwordHash" in user));
  const me = await call("GET", "/api/me", undefined, token);
  assert.equal(me.status, 200);
  assert.deepEqual(me.body, user);
  const login = await call("POST", "/api/login", { email: "grace@example.com", password: "hopper1906" });
  assert.equal(login.status, 200);
});

test("signup turns UserError into 400 {error}", async () => {
  createUser("ada@example.com", "Ada", "correct horse");
  const dup = await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "ADA@example.com", name: "Ada2", password: "correct horse" });
  assert.deepEqual([dup.status, dup.body], [400, { error: "email already registered" }]);
  const short = await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "b@example.com", name: "B", password: "short" });
  assert.deepEqual([short.status, short.body], [400, { error: "password must be at least 8 characters" }]);
  const bad = await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "not-an-email", name: "C", password: "long enough" });
  assert.deepEqual([bad.status, bad.body], [400, { error: "invalid email" }]);
});

test("signup requires email and password", async () => {
  assert.equal((await call("POST", "/api/signup", { name: "X", password: "long enough" })).status, 400);
  assert.equal((await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "x@example.com", name: "X" })).status, 400);
});
```

